1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,880
Nine numbers, arranged in a box.

2
00:00:04,690 --> 00:00:06,240
It might be the most useful

3
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:08,130
object in all of mathematics.

4
00:00:08,620 --> 00:00:10,070
It also looks like the most boring.

5
00:00:11,010 --> 00:00:12,020
But give it something to

6
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:13,800
act on, and it comes alive.

7
00:00:14,290 --> 00:00:15,840
Each column becomes an arrow.

8
00:00:16,190 --> 00:00:18,740
Space itself tilts, stretches, and

9
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:20,660
folds, and anything living in that

10
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:22,180
space goes along for the ride.

11
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:25,933
Every photo on your phone is, underneath,

12
00:00:25,987 --> 00:00:27,960
a grid of numbers like this one.

13
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:29,080
Every frame of a video

14
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:30,720
game is drawn with them.

15
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:31,800
One of them helped Google

16
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:33,453
rank the entire web.

17
00:00:33,453 --> 00:00:35,320
And a modern neural network is, for the

18
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:37,520
most part, a very long stack of them.

19
00:00:38,530 --> 00:00:39,980
And yet, most of us first

20
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:41,740
meet matrices as bookkeeping.

21
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:44,300
Rows and columns, and a strange rule

22
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:46,340
for multiplying them that you practice,

23
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:48,420
use on an exam, and quietly forget.

24
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:50,400
The rule works.

25
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:52,850
It just hides what a matrix actually is.

26
00:00:53,850 --> 00:00:55,140
So here is the claim this

27
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:56,300
video is going to earn.

28
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:58,940
A matrix is not a table you read.

29
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:00,540
It is a move you make.

30
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:02,860
A way of picking up all of space,

31
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:04,440
and setting it down somewhere new.

32
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:06,840
Once you see it that way, the

33
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:08,710
strange rules stop being strange.

34
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:10,840
Multiplying two matrices means

35
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:12,570
doing one move, and then another.

36
00:01:12,910 --> 00:01:14,800
Some moves crush space flat,

37
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:16,010
and can never be undone.

38
00:01:16,430 --> 00:01:17,800
Some leave a few special

39
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:19,040
directions untouched.

40
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:21,000
And every one of them, however

41
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:22,440
messy its numbers look, is

42
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:24,310
built from three simple motions.

43
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:27,020
We'll start with two arrows on a flat

44
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:29,180
grid, and end by taking a photograph

45
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:31,130
apart, one stretch at a time.

46
00:01:36,150 --> 00:01:37,530
First, the plain facts.

47
00:01:37,870 --> 00:01:40,020
A matrix is a rectangle of numbers.

48
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:42,240
We give its size as rows, then

49
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:45,200
columns, so this one is two by three.

50
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:47,230
And every entry has an address: which

51
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:48,950
row it sits in, and which column.

52
00:01:49,950 --> 00:01:51,700
Because it's so plain, a matrix

53
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:53,050
can hold almost anything.

54
00:01:53,380 --> 00:01:54,740
Take three equations that

55
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:56,320
share the same three unknowns.

56
00:01:56,650 --> 00:01:58,340
Strip away the letters, keep only

57
00:01:58,420 --> 00:01:59,420
the numbers in front of them,

58
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:01,230
and what's left is a matrix.

59
00:02:01,690 --> 00:02:03,130
Remember these three equations.

60
00:02:03,370 --> 00:02:04,400
We'll come back and solve them.

61
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:06,300
Or take a picture.

62
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:07,800
This one is a single

63
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:08,960
frame from a moment ago.

64
00:02:09,220 --> 00:02:10,960
Zoom in far enough and it breaks

65
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,400
into tiny squares, and each square is

66
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:15,030
just a number for how bright it is.

67
00:02:15,310 --> 00:02:17,480
A black and white picture is a matrix with

68
00:02:17,560 --> 00:02:19,670
hundreds of rows and hundreds of columns.

69
00:02:20,010 --> 00:02:21,090
Keep this one in mind, too.

70
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:23,900
So a matrix can be a table.

71
00:02:24,220 --> 00:02:25,840
But that leaves a strange question.

72
00:02:26,130 --> 00:02:27,580
A table of exam grades

73
00:02:27,660 --> 00:02:28,750
can't rotate anything.

74
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,110
A list of prices can't tilt a die.

75
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:33,180
So how did nine numbers, at

76
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:34,540
the start of this video, pick

77
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:36,270
up a whole space and turn it?

78
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:38,480
To answer that, we have to

79
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:40,000
stop reading the numbers, and

80
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:41,320
start watching what they do.

81
00:02:43,130 --> 00:02:44,940
Picture a flat grid, and an

82
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:46,420
arrow starting from its center.

83
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:49,020
That arrow is a vector, and you can

84
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:50,890
describe it by where its tip sits.

85
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:53,380
Two steps over, one step up.

86
00:02:54,410 --> 00:02:55,740
Now imagine a machine.

87
00:02:55,990 --> 00:02:57,600
You feed it an arrow, and it

88
00:02:57,680 --> 00:02:59,070
hands you back a different arrow.

89
00:02:59,590 --> 00:03:01,260
Arrow in, arrow out.

90
00:03:02,290 --> 00:03:04,847
Watching one arrow at a time gets slow.

91
00:03:04,873 --> 00:03:05,940
So let's feed the machine

92
00:03:06,020 --> 00:03:07,280
every arrow at once.

93
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:09,860
Every point on the grid slides to its new

94
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:12,020
home, and the grid lines travel with them.

95
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:14,440
Most machines would tangle

96
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:15,620
the grid into a mess.

97
00:03:15,930 --> 00:03:17,160
The ones that matrices

98
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:18,950
describe follow three rules.

99
00:03:19,330 --> 00:03:20,960
Straight lines stay straight.

100
00:03:21,490 --> 00:03:24,147
Evenly spaced lines stay evenly spaced.

101
00:03:24,173 --> 00:03:25,740
And the center stays where it is.

102
00:03:26,210 --> 00:03:28,100
Moves like these are called linear.

103
00:03:29,110 --> 00:03:30,540
Inside those rules, there's a

104
00:03:30,620 --> 00:03:31,820
surprising amount of freedom.

105
00:03:32,230 --> 00:03:33,860
You can rotate the whole plane.

106
00:03:34,310 --> 00:03:35,740
You can stretch it, more in

107
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:36,900
one direction than the other.

108
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:39,180
You can shear it, sliding each

109
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:40,460
row a little further than the

110
00:03:40,540 --> 00:03:42,250
one below, like a deck of cards.

111
00:03:42,670 --> 00:03:44,390
You can flip it, as if in a mirror.

112
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:46,060
You can even squash it

113
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:47,750
flat, onto a single line.

114
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:50,400
What you can't do is bend the grid

115
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:52,400
lines into curves, or drag the

116
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:54,030
whole plane away from its center.

117
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:56,180
And all of this carries

118
00:03:56,260 --> 00:03:57,550
over to three dimensions.

119
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:00,700
A die can be turned, stretched, sheared,

120
00:04:00,740 --> 00:04:02,740
and flattened, and the grid of space

121
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:04,950
moves right along with it, cell for cell.

122
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:07,130
So that's the machine.

123
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:08,860
But where do the numbers come in?

124
00:04:10,770 --> 00:04:12,420
This is the idea that everything

125
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:13,830
else in this video grows out of.

126
00:04:14,870 --> 00:04:16,430
Look at two special arrows.

127
00:04:16,830 --> 00:04:18,740
One step to the right, called i hat.

128
00:04:19,140 --> 00:04:21,020
And one step up, called j hat.

129
00:04:21,510 --> 00:04:22,840
Every other arrow can be

130
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:23,990
built out of these two.

131
00:04:24,410 --> 00:04:26,440
Our arrow from before is two copies

132
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:28,730
of i hat, plus one copy of j hat.

133
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:30,840
Now apply a move.

134
00:04:31,250 --> 00:04:32,860
Because the grid lines stay parallel

135
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:35,470
and evenly spaced, that recipe survives.

136
00:04:35,860 --> 00:04:37,820
The new arrow is still two copies

137
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:39,740
of wherever i hat landed, plus

138
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:41,690
one copy of wherever j hat landed.

139
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:44,200
Think about how much that gives you.

140
00:04:44,590 --> 00:04:45,840
To know where every single

141
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:47,330
point in the plane goes, you

142
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:49,300
only need to follow two arrows.

143
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:51,570
So write down where they land.

144
00:04:51,960 --> 00:04:52,930
Where i hat lands

145
00:04:52,980 --> 00:04:54,270
becomes the first column.

146
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:56,290
Where j hat lands becomes the second.

147
00:04:56,690 --> 00:04:58,600
Those two columns are the matrix.

148
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:00,390
Nothing more, nothing less.

149
00:05:01,260 --> 00:05:03,480
And multiplying a matrix by a vector is

150
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:05,440
just that recipe, written in numbers.

151
00:05:05,810 --> 00:05:07,080
Scale the first column by

152
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:08,240
the vector's first number.

153
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:09,720
Scale the second column

154
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:10,800
by its second number.

155
00:05:11,020 --> 00:05:13,380
Add the two. That's where the arrow lands.

156
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:15,500
Which means you can now read a

157
00:05:15,740 --> 00:05:17,250
matrix the way you'd read a map.

158
00:05:17,430 --> 00:05:18,620
Try this one.

159
00:05:18,670 --> 00:05:20,780
Its first column says i hat

160
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:22,410
lands pointing straight up.

161
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:26,087
Its second says j hat lands pointing left.

162
00:05:26,193 --> 00:05:27,193
Before the grid moves,

163
00:05:27,330 --> 00:05:28,330
what do you think it does?

164
00:05:31,890 --> 00:05:33,380
It turns the whole plane a

165
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:35,290
quarter turn, counterclockwise.

166
00:05:36,210 --> 00:05:38,280
Three dimensions work exactly the same

167
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:40,820
way, with three arrows and three columns.

168
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:42,680
So now we can decode the

169
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:44,060
box from the very beginning.

170
00:05:44,330 --> 00:05:46,600
The first column is where i hat goes.

171
00:05:46,950 --> 00:05:48,430
The second, j hat.

172
00:05:48,730 --> 00:05:50,120
The third is where k hat

173
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:51,900
goes, the arrow pointing up.

174
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:53,560
Nine numbers.

175
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:54,640
Three arrows.

176
00:05:55,180 --> 00:05:56,180
This is how a video

177
00:05:56,260 --> 00:05:57,260
game turns a character.

178
00:05:57,460 --> 00:05:59,340
The character is built from thousands

179
00:05:59,420 --> 00:06:01,100
of corner points, and every one

180
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:02,860
of them gets multiplied by the same

181
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:05,180
small matrix, fresh for every frame.

182
00:06:07,110 --> 00:06:08,120
What happens if you make

183
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:09,640
one move, and then another?

184
00:06:10,130 --> 00:06:12,680
Shear the plane, and then rotate it.

185
00:06:13,180 --> 00:06:14,610
The grid still ends up straight,

186
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:16,490
evenly spaced, and centered.

187
00:06:16,900 --> 00:06:19,100
So the combined motion is itself a

188
00:06:19,260 --> 00:06:21,590
single move, and it has its own matrix.

189
00:06:21,810 --> 00:06:23,100
To find it, we just

190
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:24,560
follow i hat and j hat.

191
00:06:25,530 --> 00:06:27,600
The shear leaves i hat where it is, and

192
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:29,470
then the rotation sends it straight up.

193
00:06:29,690 --> 00:06:30,960
That's the first column

194
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:32,040
of the combined move.

195
00:06:32,450 --> 00:06:34,160
The shear tips j hat onto the

196
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:36,240
diagonal, and the rotation carries

197
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:38,060
that diagonal up and to the left.

198
00:06:38,270 --> 00:06:39,590
That's the second column.

199
00:06:40,470 --> 00:06:42,153
Look at what we just did.

200
00:06:42,153 --> 00:06:43,680
To get each column of the result,

201
00:06:43,970 --> 00:06:45,460
we pushed a column of the first

202
00:06:45,740 --> 00:06:47,020
matrix through the second one.

203
00:06:47,420 --> 00:06:48,820
Write that out with numbers, and

204
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:50,340
out comes the row times column

205
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:51,730
rule from the start of the video.

206
00:06:52,120 --> 00:06:53,220
Nobody invented that

207
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:54,847
rule to torture students.

208
00:06:54,873 --> 00:06:56,180
It's simply what two moves in

209
00:06:56,220 --> 00:06:57,740
a row look like, written down.

210
00:06:58,650 --> 00:06:59,800
We write the rotation on the

211
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:01,720
left, because it acts second, the

212
00:07:01,840 --> 00:07:03,750
same way you'd write f of g of x.

213
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:05,580
Which raises a fair question.

214
00:07:05,980 --> 00:07:06,980
Does the order matter?

215
00:07:07,340 --> 00:07:09,747
If you rotate first and shear second,

216
00:07:09,773 --> 00:07:11,030
do you end up in the same place?

217
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:15,200
You don't.

218
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:17,580
The two grids land in different places.

219
00:07:17,970 --> 00:07:19,787
And real objects agree.

220
00:07:19,787 --> 00:07:21,627
Turn a die a quarter turn forward,

221
00:07:21,653 --> 00:07:23,060
and then a quarter turn to the side.

222
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:24,640
Now start over, and do

223
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:25,720
it in the other order.

224
00:07:25,890 --> 00:07:27,820
A different face ends up on top.

225
00:07:28,710 --> 00:07:30,260
With matrices, the order

226
00:07:30,340 --> 00:07:31,440
is part of the move.

227
00:07:32,310 --> 00:07:34,400
This idea, stacking moves, is

228
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:35,930
the heart of a neural network.

229
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:38,320
Each layer takes a list of numbers,

230
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:40,360
multiplies it by a matrix, shifts it

231
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:42,440
a little, and then bends the result,

232
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:44,280
squashing the largest values.

233
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:46,900
That bend matters more than it looks.

234
00:07:47,110 --> 00:07:49,500
Without it, ten layers of matrices

235
00:07:49,620 --> 00:07:51,260
would multiply together into just

236
00:07:51,420 --> 00:07:53,870
one matrix, a single straight move.

237
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:55,980
And no single straight move

238
00:07:56,100 --> 00:07:57,820
can untangle these two spirals.

239
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:00,560
With the bends, each layer

240
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:01,820
folds space a little.

241
00:08:02,370 --> 00:08:04,240
Fold by fold, the spirals pull

242
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:06,000
apart, until the two colors

243
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:07,510
can be cleanly told apart.

244
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:10,020
Every move we've made so far

245
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:11,200
could be played backwards.

246
00:08:11,490 --> 00:08:13,860
But that isn't always possible.

247
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:16,400
Here's the matrix we'll carry

248
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:17,560
through the rest of the video.

249
00:08:17,990 --> 00:08:20,587
It tilts and stretches the grid.

250
00:08:20,587 --> 00:08:22,240
To undo it, you play the move in

251
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:24,587
reverse, and that reverse move has a

252
00:08:24,693 --> 00:08:26,670
matrix of its own, called the inverse.

253
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:29,080
Do the move, then the inverse,

254
00:08:29,290 --> 00:08:31,467
and every point comes home.

255
00:08:31,653 --> 00:08:32,720
Together they make the move that

256
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:34,740
does nothing at all, the identity.

257
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:37,140
Now watch the little unit

258
00:08:37,220 --> 00:08:38,660
square during the move.

259
00:08:38,660 --> 00:08:40,800
Its area grows by a factor of four.

260
00:08:41,350 --> 00:08:43,220
That one number, the stretch in

261
00:08:43,300 --> 00:08:45,040
area, is called the determinant.

262
00:08:45,270 --> 00:08:47,487
It has a whole video of its own.

263
00:08:47,987 --> 00:08:49,620
For now, one question matters.

264
00:08:50,020 --> 00:08:51,890
What happens when that number is zero?

265
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:54,900
Then the area becomes nothing.

266
00:08:55,310 --> 00:08:56,380
The whole plane gets

267
00:08:56,460 --> 00:08:58,807
squashed onto a single line.

268
00:08:58,913 --> 00:09:00,000
And something is lost.

269
00:09:00,450 --> 00:09:01,610
Look at these different points.

270
00:09:01,970 --> 00:09:04,240
They all land on exactly the same spot.

271
00:09:04,580 --> 00:09:06,380
Once they're there, nothing can tell

272
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:08,830
them apart again, so no move can undo it.

273
00:09:09,750 --> 00:09:11,920
One entire line of points gets

274
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:13,640
crushed into the center itself.

275
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:16,200
That line is called the null space.

276
00:09:16,410 --> 00:09:18,320
And everything that survives lands

277
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:20,240
on the output line, called the column

278
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:22,600
space: every place the move can reach.

279
00:09:23,570 --> 00:09:24,953
You've seen this happen.

280
00:09:25,007 --> 00:09:26,930
A shadow is exactly this kind of move.

281
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:29,460
Sunlight flattens a three dimensional

282
00:09:29,620 --> 00:09:31,380
die onto the ground, and every

283
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:32,980
point along a single ray of light

284
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:35,753
ends up on the same spot of shadow.

285
00:09:35,753 --> 00:09:36,820
The direction of the light

286
00:09:36,900 --> 00:09:37,790
is the null space.

287
00:09:38,070 --> 00:09:39,300
That's why you can't rebuild

288
00:09:39,380 --> 00:09:41,513
the die from its shadow alone.

289
00:09:41,567 --> 00:09:42,580
A photograph does something

290
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:44,527
similar, which is why depth in a

291
00:09:44,633 --> 00:09:46,100
single photo has to be inferred

292
00:09:46,220 --> 00:09:48,220
from clues, never read off directly.

293
00:09:49,170 --> 00:09:50,280
Now, about those three

294
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:51,400
equations from the start.

295
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:53,000
Solving them is really

296
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:54,440
a question about a move.

297
00:09:54,710 --> 00:09:57,080
Some matrix moves space, and we

298
00:09:57,160 --> 00:09:58,370
know where an arrow ended up.

299
00:09:58,730 --> 00:10:00,680
Which arrow was sent there?

300
00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:02,460
In two dimensions, each

301
00:10:02,580 --> 00:10:04,140
equation is a line, and the

302
00:10:04,220 --> 00:10:05,770
answer is where the lines cross.

303
00:10:06,080 --> 00:10:08,220
Seen as a move, it's the one arrow

304
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:10,010
that lands exactly on the target.

305
00:10:11,130 --> 00:10:12,960
With three unknowns, each equation

306
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:15,680
becomes a flat plane in space, and three

307
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:17,910
planes usually meet at a single point.

308
00:10:18,150 --> 00:10:19,660
That point is the solution.

309
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:21,540
But if the determinant is

310
00:10:21,740 --> 00:10:23,810
zero, space has been flattened.

311
00:10:24,110 --> 00:10:26,100
Then the planes either share infinitely

312
00:10:26,180 --> 00:10:28,340
many points, or they miss each other

313
00:10:28,500 --> 00:10:30,460
entirely, and there's no answer at all.

314
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:32,740
Either way, you can't simply

315
00:10:32,820 --> 00:10:33,920
run the move backwards.

316
00:10:34,330 --> 00:10:35,860
That's what a zero determinant has

317
00:10:35,940 --> 00:10:37,510
been warning you about all along.

318
00:10:39,640 --> 00:10:40,890
Back to our running example.

319
00:10:40,990 --> 00:10:43,120
This time, watch single arrows

320
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:44,640
instead of the whole grid.

321
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:46,080
Most arrows get knocked

322
00:10:46,160 --> 00:10:47,160
off their own line.

323
00:10:47,420 --> 00:10:48,840
They come out pointing somewhere new.

324
00:10:49,670 --> 00:10:50,900
But sweep around the

325
00:10:51,180 --> 00:10:52,450
circle, and watch closely.

326
00:10:53,010 --> 00:10:55,590
Here, the output lines up with the input.

327
00:10:56,110 --> 00:10:57,300
This arrow only gets

328
00:10:57,380 --> 00:10:59,460
stretched, by a factor of four.

329
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:00,680
And here's another one.

330
00:11:00,910 --> 00:11:02,460
This one isn't stretched at all.

331
00:11:03,610 --> 00:11:05,400
These special directions are called

332
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:07,320
eigenvectors, and how much each

333
00:11:07,400 --> 00:11:09,370
one stretches is its eigenvalue.

334
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:11,320
They're the skeleton of the move.

335
00:11:11,730 --> 00:11:13,880
Along them, a complicated matrix

336
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:15,720
behaves like plain multiplication

337
00:11:15,760 --> 00:11:16,760
by a single number.

338
00:11:17,790 --> 00:11:18,790
A quick test.

339
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:20,990
Take a rotation of the flat plane.

340
00:11:21,290 --> 00:11:23,110
Which arrows stay on their own line?

341
00:11:26,740 --> 00:11:28,870
None of them. Every arrow turns.

342
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:31,913
But in three dimensions, it's different.

343
00:11:31,913 --> 00:11:33,060
Remember the die, turned

344
00:11:33,140 --> 00:11:34,170
one way and then the other?

345
00:11:34,550 --> 00:11:36,900
Those two quarter turns combine into

346
00:11:37,140 --> 00:11:39,280
a single turn, about a tilted axis.

347
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:42,060
Arrows along that axis don't move at all.

348
00:11:42,410 --> 00:11:44,100
Every rotation in space

349
00:11:44,180 --> 00:11:45,370
has an axis like this.

350
00:11:45,620 --> 00:11:47,480
The axis is an eigenvector,

351
00:11:47,710 --> 00:11:49,300
with an eigenvalue of one.

352
00:11:50,300 --> 00:11:51,800
Eigenvectors also tell you

353
00:11:51,880 --> 00:11:53,060
where things are heading.

354
00:11:53,350 --> 00:11:55,000
Take a cloud of points, and apply

355
00:11:55,120 --> 00:11:57,920
the same matrix again, and again.

356
00:11:57,960 --> 00:11:59,640
Each time, the direction with the

357
00:11:59,720 --> 00:12:02,120
biggest stretch wins a little more.

358
00:12:02,120 --> 00:12:03,640
After just a few rounds, the

359
00:12:03,720 --> 00:12:06,277
whole cloud has lined up along it.

360
00:12:06,333 --> 00:12:08,240
Apply a matrix over and over,

361
00:12:08,280 --> 00:12:09,560
and everything drifts toward

362
00:12:09,640 --> 00:12:11,020
its strongest eigenvector.

363
00:12:11,510 --> 00:12:13,080
That simple fact once

364
00:12:13,160 --> 00:12:14,760
organized the entire web.

365
00:12:15,820 --> 00:12:17,420
Here's a tiny web of seven

366
00:12:17,780 --> 00:12:19,580
pages, and thousands of imaginary

367
00:12:19,660 --> 00:12:21,210
readers clicking links at random.

368
00:12:21,590 --> 00:12:23,820
Every so often, a reader gets bored

369
00:12:23,900 --> 00:12:26,010
and jumps to a random page instead.

370
00:12:26,730 --> 00:12:28,240
Each round of clicks is one

371
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:30,000
multiplication by a matrix that

372
00:12:30,080 --> 00:12:31,690
shuffles readers between pages.

373
00:12:32,150 --> 00:12:33,870
Run it, and the crowd settles.

374
00:12:34,180 --> 00:12:36,320
Some pages end up holding far more

375
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:37,947
readers than others, and it doesn't

376
00:12:38,053 --> 00:12:39,180
matter where everyone started.

377
00:12:40,190 --> 00:12:41,700
That settled crowd is an

378
00:12:41,780 --> 00:12:43,647
eigenvector of the click matrix,

379
00:12:43,673 --> 00:12:45,090
with an eigenvalue of one.

380
00:12:45,490 --> 00:12:47,200
The shuffle no longer changes it.

381
00:12:47,570 --> 00:12:49,060
This was the idea behind

382
00:12:49,140 --> 00:12:50,140
Google's PageRank.

383
00:12:50,650 --> 00:12:51,910
And look at these two pages.

384
00:12:52,320 --> 00:12:54,820
Each has just one incoming link.

385
00:12:54,820 --> 00:12:55,940
But one of those links comes

386
00:12:56,020 --> 00:12:57,753
from the most popular page, and

387
00:12:57,940 --> 00:12:59,570
that makes it count for far more.

388
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:03,120
There's one more thing to see, and

389
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:05,390
it's the deepest idea in this video.

390
00:13:06,210 --> 00:13:07,727
Draw a circle of arrows,

391
00:13:07,753 --> 00:13:09,230
and apply our matrix.

392
00:13:09,510 --> 00:13:11,140
The circle becomes an ellipse.

393
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:12,850
That's no accident.

394
00:13:13,060 --> 00:13:14,420
Every matrix like this one

395
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:16,740
turns a circle into an ellipse.

396
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:18,440
An ellipse has a longest

397
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:20,160
direction and a shortest one,

398
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:21,450
at right angles to each other.

399
00:13:21,590 --> 00:13:23,800
Follow them back, and you find two

400
00:13:23,880 --> 00:13:26,093
input arrows, also at right angles,

401
00:13:26,147 --> 00:13:28,090
that land exactly on those axes.

402
00:13:28,930 --> 00:13:30,380
So here's what the matrix

403
00:13:30,460 --> 00:13:32,090
really does, in three steps.

404
00:13:32,650 --> 00:13:35,180
First, rotate, so those two input

405
00:13:35,260 --> 00:13:37,260
arrows line up with the grid.

406
00:13:37,260 --> 00:13:39,280
Then stretch along the grid, more

407
00:13:39,420 --> 00:13:40,970
in one direction than the other.

408
00:13:41,240 --> 00:13:42,860
Then rotate again, into

409
00:13:42,940 --> 00:13:44,120
the final position.

410
00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:46,490
Rotate, stretch, rotate.

411
00:13:46,830 --> 00:13:49,020
Sometimes one of those turns includes a

412
00:13:49,140 --> 00:13:51,070
mirror flip, but that's the whole story.

413
00:13:51,910 --> 00:13:53,920
This is called the singular value

414
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:55,840
decomposition, and the amounts of

415
00:13:55,920 --> 00:13:58,400
stretch are called singular values.

416
00:13:58,400 --> 00:13:59,880
It works for every matrix,

417
00:13:59,920 --> 00:14:01,340
of any shape or size.

418
00:14:01,630 --> 00:14:04,000
In three dimensions, a sphere becomes an

419
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:06,960
ellipsoid with three perpendicular axes.

420
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:08,720
However messy the numbers, the move

421
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:11,350
underneath is always this simple.

422
00:14:12,130 --> 00:14:13,460
Now, remember the picture from the

423
00:14:13,580 --> 00:14:15,680
beginning, the grid of brightness values?

424
00:14:15,880 --> 00:14:17,460
It's a matrix too, so it

425
00:14:17,580 --> 00:14:18,640
has this same structure.

426
00:14:18,870 --> 00:14:20,900
Two rotations, and a list of

427
00:14:20,980 --> 00:14:23,020
stretches, from biggest to smallest.

428
00:14:23,780 --> 00:14:25,400
Each stretch contributes

429
00:14:25,480 --> 00:14:26,540
one layer to the picture.

430
00:14:26,780 --> 00:14:28,680
And each layer is about as simple

431
00:14:28,760 --> 00:14:30,920
as an image can be: a single column

432
00:14:30,960 --> 00:14:32,660
of numbers, times a single row.

433
00:14:33,140 --> 00:14:34,940
Here's the biggest layer on its own.

434
00:14:35,190 --> 00:14:36,400
Just a soft blur.

435
00:14:37,080 --> 00:14:39,110
Add the second, and the third.

436
00:14:39,420 --> 00:14:41,120
By ten, you can make out the die.

437
00:14:41,410 --> 00:14:44,440
By thirty, it's close to the original.

438
00:14:44,440 --> 00:14:46,280
Thirty layers out of hundreds, using

439
00:14:46,360 --> 00:14:48,387
about an eighth of the numbers.

440
00:14:48,387 --> 00:14:49,387
The small stretches were

441
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:51,130
mostly fine detail, and noise.

442
00:14:51,950 --> 00:14:54,887
This is a classic way to compress images,

443
00:14:54,913 --> 00:14:56,620
and the same idea finds the patterns

444
00:14:56,700 --> 00:14:59,313
hiding in huge tables of measurements.

445
00:14:59,367 --> 00:15:00,700
So the table and the machine

446
00:15:00,820 --> 00:15:02,190
were never two different things.

447
00:15:02,440 --> 00:15:05,150
A grid of numbers is a move.

448
00:15:05,153 --> 00:15:06,380
And its biggest stretches

449
00:15:06,420 --> 00:15:07,420
are what matter most.

450
00:15:09,310 --> 00:15:10,760
Let's look at that box of

451
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:12,947
nine numbers one last time.

452
00:15:13,447 --> 00:15:15,740
Its columns are where the axes land.

453
00:15:15,740 --> 00:15:17,340
Multiplying it by another matrix

454
00:15:17,420 --> 00:15:18,990
means one move after another.

455
00:15:19,350 --> 00:15:21,580
Its determinant tells you how much volume

456
00:15:21,660 --> 00:15:23,780
grows, and whether the move can be undone.

457
00:15:24,050 --> 00:15:26,060
Its eigenvectors are the directions

458
00:15:26,140 --> 00:15:27,390
it leaves on their own line.

459
00:15:27,610 --> 00:15:30,010
And underneath it all, it's a rotation,

460
00:15:30,270 --> 00:15:32,230
a stretch, and another rotation.

461
00:15:33,210 --> 00:15:34,210
One last thought.

462
00:15:34,580 --> 00:15:35,920
These nine numbers aren't

463
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:37,150
really the move itself.

464
00:15:37,360 --> 00:15:39,040
They're what the move looks like from

465
00:15:39,160 --> 00:15:41,360
where we're standing, measured along our

466
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:45,040
own choice of i hat, j hat, and k hat.

467
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:47,040
Pick three different arrows, and the very

468
00:15:47,120 --> 00:15:49,380
same move gets nine different numbers.

469
00:15:50,140 --> 00:15:51,140
The numbers change.

470
00:15:51,490 --> 00:15:52,520
The move doesn't.

471
00:15:53,740 --> 00:15:54,840
If you'd like to see one of those

472
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:56,600
numbers up close, the determinant,

473
00:15:56,970 --> 00:15:58,320
that video is right here.

474
00:15:58,900 --> 00:15:59,960
Thanks for watching.
